# OP-209 `a2a show`, OP-210 `a2a thread`: FOLDED-STATE observable at the
# OP-contract level (exact string per cmd_show.go's own format:
# "<id> <type> (<state>) — <title>").
exec a2a show XR-axon-demo
stdout 'XR-axon-demo requirement \(published\) — t'

# `a2a thread` is queried by THREAD id, not by artifact id. Before P46 this
# script passed XR-axon-demo's own id as the "thread id" and asserted one
# line came back — spec 46 records that as defect D7: the single test
# claiming to cover this verb asserted a value that is not a thread id at
# all, so it could not have caught a real thread bug, and even once fixed to
# query a real thread it still only asserted one artifact, no events, no
# ordering.
#
# spec 46 §T7 (P46 wave 6) replaces both gaps with a real chain: a question
# (beta -> gamma), an acknowledge event, a response (gamma -> beta, carrying
# the parent's own thread — never re-derived) and a verify — from TWO
# systems, across FOUR real commits (internal/e2e/thread_chain_test.go's
# seedThreadChainFixture; internal/e2e/helpers_test.go's seedOriginExtras
# pushes it to this script's shared origin before the mirror is cloned).
# Deliberately beta/gamma, not axon: this origin is shared by every T3
# script and every script runs AS axon — a thread axon has no part in never
# touches axon's own inbox/outbox/statusline (see this phase's own report:
# an earlier axon-rooted version of this fixture broke both scripts).
#
# D3: the QUESTION transcripts strictly BEFORE the RESPONSE. Before this
# wave's fix, a response carries no event of its own until it is verified
# (the `respond` transition is recorded against the QUESTION's subject, not
# the response's — cmd_lifecycle.go's own RespondCommand.Run), so the old
# LatestEventAt-ordered reader sorted it FIRST; the real per-commit sequence
# (mirror.go's commitOrder) puts it fourth, after the question, its two
# events, and the response's own commit.
#
# D2: event rows are present at all — submit, acknowledge, respond, verify —
# the old reader listed artifacts only.
exec a2a thread thread:beta-20260722-c9h1
stdout '(?s)artifact\tbeta\tquestion\tXQ-beta-20260722-q1a2\t.*artifact\tgamma\tresponse\tXS-gamma-20260722-r3b4\t'
stdout 'event\tbeta\tsubmit\tXQ-beta-20260722-q1a2'
stdout 'event\tgamma\tacknowledge\tXQ-beta-20260722-q1a2'
stdout 'event\tgamma\trespond\tXQ-beta-20260722-q1a2'
stdout 'event\tbeta\tverify\tXS-gamma-20260722-r3b4'

# OPEN names who OWES the next move, which is beta alone: gamma has already
# answered, and the ball is with the sender to verify and close (or dispute).
#
# This line used to read `waiting_on=beta,gamma`, justified as "both parties
# actually able to act next". That is the liveness predicate, not the pendency
# one — gamma's "respond again" (D-017's multi-response allowance) stays LEGAL
# from `responded` and is still listed in `next=` below, but nobody is waiting
# for it. P11 W1 separated the two, and this fixture had encoded the
# conflation. If a dispute reopens the exchange, gamma owes again and this line
# changes with it. Do not "restore" the second name: next= is where every legal
# move remains visible.
stdout 'XQ-beta-20260722-q1a2\tresponded\twaiting_on=beta\tnext=respond:gamma;close:beta;supersede:beta'

# US-6/D6: a member artifact id resolves to the SAME thread as the thread id
# itself — --json makes the exact field values unambiguous.
exec a2a thread XS-gamma-20260722-r3b4 --json
stdout '"thread": "thread:beta-20260722-c9h1"'
stdout '"resolved_from": "XS-gamma-20260722-r3b4"'

# an unknown ref exits 1, never a silent empty success (D6) — exact exit
# code via the `exec sh -c` idiom (feedback_intake_guards.txtar,
# statusline.txtar).
exec sh -c 'a2a thread thread:nowhere-20260101-0000; echo "exit=$?"'
stdout 'exit=1'
